Set 1: Run-Loop Coalescing & Layout Thrashing

Interviewer Question 1:

"In Ember.js applications handling high-frequency user actions, how does the Run Loop coordinate asynchronous updates, and how have you resolved layout thrashing caused by improper queue scheduling?"

Candidate Answer 1:

"Ember's Run Loop, powered by Backburner.js, coordinates asynchronous DOM reads and writes by batching them across explicit priority queues: sync, actions, router, render, afterRender, and destroy. Its primary purpose is to avoid layout thrashing by guaranteeing that state mutations and calculations happen before the browser attempts to compute styles and paint. In Zoho Books line-item tables, thrashing occurred when engineers mixed reads and writes across queues. We resolved this by strictly decoupling layout reads from mutations, using requestAnimationFrame outside the run loop queues to measure sizes, and deferring state writes via microtasks so measurement and mutation never interleave in the same tick."

Interviewer Follow-up Probe:

"Specifically, what happens under the hood if a developer reads DOM dimensions inside the afterRender queue while dispatching a tracked property mutation?"

Candidate Answer 2:

"When you read a geometric property like offsetHeight inside afterRender, the browser is forced to pause and compute a synchronous layout reflow. If that callback simultaneously mutates an @tracked property, Ember immediately schedules a brand new cycle of work back into the actions or sync queue. This creates an uncoordinated cascade of re-renders inside a single frame, spiking frame times well past 16 milliseconds and causing visible UI stutter."

Set 2: Auto-Tracking Mechanics vs. Classical Computed Properties

Interviewer Question 1:

"Explain the fundamental shift from Ember Classic computed properties to Ember Octane tracked properties. What actually changes in memory?"

Candidate Answer 1:

"Classic computed properties relied on explicit, string-based dependency paths like Ember.computed('items.@each.price'). To make that work, Ember had to register explicit event listeners on every intermediate object along that path. Octane’s @tracked completely changes this by using autotracking powered by a global transaction counter and revision tags. When a getter runs, Ember notes which tracked properties were read during execution and stores their current revision number. No listeners are attached to the data models at all. Recalculation only triggers if a tag’s counter increments."

Interviewer Follow-up Probe:

"Why did Classic computed properties cause memory retention issues in long-lived sessions compared to Octane's revision tag system?"

Candidate Answer 2:

"In long-lived ERP sessions running 8 to 10 hours, the listener graphs created by Classic computed properties kept objects pinned in memory. If a parent view or service listened to a child record, the garbage collector could not reclaim that child even after its component was destroyed. With Octane’s revision tag system, because there are zero listener bindings between objects, models can be garbage collected as soon as the component unmounts, cutting memory usage by over 40% in large transactional tables."

Set 3: Glimmer VM Bytecode Execution vs. React VDOM Reconciliation

Interviewer Question 1:

"Compare Glimmer VM's template compilation and wire-format execution with React's Virtual DOM diffing. Why is Glimmer particularly efficient for tabular enterprise interfaces?"

Candidate Answer 1:

"React reconciles state by constructing an entirely new tree of plain JavaScript objects representing the UI and comparing it recursively against the previous tree. Glimmer VM takes a different approach: at build time, it compiles Ember templates into compact bytecode instructions, completely separating the static HTML structure from dynamic expressions. At runtime, the Glimmer VM only evaluates the dynamic slots, completely bypassing the static tags."

Interviewer Follow-up Probe:

"When a single cell updates in a 1,000-row table, what is the exact difference in runtime memory allocations between the two engines?"

Candidate Answer 2:

"In React, updating a single cell requires re-evaluating the parent component and generating new Virtual DOM nodes across the row or table unless you wrap everything in React.memo. In Glimmer, the VM jumps directly to the memory address of that single dynamic expression, compares its revision tag, and mutates only that specific DOM text node. Glimmer allocates zero objects for the surrounding static table tags, which keeps heap allocations flat during rapid data entry."

