Set 25: Application-Level Deadlock Prevention in Java

Interviewer Question 1:

"How do concurrent Java threads produce deadlocks during multi-account fund transfers, and how do you design code to make deadlocks mathematically impossible?"

Candidate Answer 1:

"Deadlocks happen when two threads acquire locks in reverse order. If Thread 1 transfers funds from Account A to Account B, it locks A then B. If Thread 2 simultaneously transfers funds from Account B to Account A, it locks B then A. Both threads block waiting for each other's lock, causing a permanent circular-wait deadlock."

Interviewer Follow-up Probe:

"Why is dynamic lock acquisition based on natural business order dangerous, and what is the code-level solution?"

Candidate Answer 2:

"Natural business order depends on unpredictable user input. We eliminate deadlocks mathematically by enforcing a strict Global Lock Hierarchy: resources must always be locked in ascending order of their unique primary IDs, regardless of the transfer direction:

Account first = from.getId() < to.getId() ? from : to;
Account second = from.getId() < to.getId() ? to : from;
synchronized(first) {
    synchronized(second) {
        // safe transfer logic
    }
}


Because every thread acquires locks in identical numerical order, circular wait conditions cannot occur."

