Set 43: HTTP/2 vs HTTP/3 Multiplexing in Enterprise Asset Delivery

Interviewer Question 1:

"How do HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 multiplexing affect asset bundling compared to legacy HTTP/1.1 practices?"

Candidate Answer 1:

"HTTP/1.1 limited browsers to six concurrent TCP connections per origin, forcing developers to bundle code into monolithic files and combine images into sprites. HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 introduced multiplexing, allowing hundreds of concurrent requests over a single connection. This allows us to break applications into smaller, domain-specific chunks that cache independently without connection overhead."

Interviewer Follow-up Probe:

"What is Head-of-Line (HoL) Blocking at the TCP transport layer, and how does HTTP/3 solve it?"

Candidate Answer 2:

"In HTTP/2, all multiplexed streams share a single underlying TCP connection. If a single network packet is dropped, TCP pauses all streams on that connection until the missing packet is retransmitted. HTTP/3 runs over QUIC, using UDP. Each stream is managed independently at the transport layer, so a dropped packet on one asset stream does not stall other streams, ensuring fast, reliable loading on lossy enterprise Wi-Fi networks."

